<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Popol Vuh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1550s</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Popol Vuh, 'Council Book', is the sacred narrative of the K'iche' Maya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Written in K'iche' in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet by anonymous nobles in the 1550s, it recounts the creation of the world, the exploits of the Hero Twins Hunahpu and Xbalanque, and the history of the K'iche' lineages. It survives in a copy made by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> Francisco Ximénez around 1701.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/popol-vuh.jpg" alt="Popol Vuh" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Ximénez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Popol_vuh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
